<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bogor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Buitenzorg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bogor is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, about 60 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, known in the colonial period as Buitenzorg. From the eighteenth century the governors-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> had a residence there, and the botanical garden founded beside the palace in 1817 became a leading centre of tropical science. The palace is now one of the official residences of the President of Indonesia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bogor.jpg" alt="Bogor" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nafisathallah · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istana_presiden_republik_Indonesia_di_Bogor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bogor is a landlocked city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>. It spreads over a basin near the volcanoes of Salak and Mount Gede, at an average elevation of 265 metres. The soils are volcanic and the area is highly seismic. It has a tropical rainforest climate, more humid and rainy than much of West Java, which gives it the nickname Rain City. Temperatures are lower than on the coast, which made it a resting place for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> officials during the colonial period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first mention of a settlement at the site dates to the 5th century, when the area belonged to Tarumanagara. After defeats by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> it became the Sunda Kingdom, and in 669 its capital was built between the parallel rivers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciliwung-river">Ciliwung</a> and Cisadane. It was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakuan-pajajaran">Pakuan Pajajaran</a> and became one of the largest cities of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, with a population reaching 48,000. Its chronicles were written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallava-script">Pallava script</a> on rock steles called prasasti.</p><p>In the second half of the 17th century Pakuan was abandoned, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>, while formally under the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a>, passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, a transfer made formal on 17 April 1684. The company sent the Sundanese lieutenant Tanoejiwa in 1687 to develop the area. The eruption of Mount Salak on 4 and 5 January 1699 damaged the settlement but the fires cleared land for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantations, and in 1701 the villages were combined into a district headed by Tanoejiwa.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> period the city was called Buitenzorg, meaning without worries in Dutch, and was the summer residence of the Governor-General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. Its botanical garden, founded in 1817, drew naturalists from abroad. Japanese forces occupied it from 6 March 1942 and gave it the name Bogor to promote anti-Dutch feeling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Bogor Botanical Garden, one of the oldest and largest in the world, covers 87 hectares and holds more than 6,000 species of tropical plants. The former residence of the governor-general is now a palace of the President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. The city is a scientific and educational centre, largely because of institutions founded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> period to improve colonial agriculture, and is home to IPB University. It had 1,043,070 people at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bogor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A governor-general, residing at Batavia and at <b>Buitenzorg</b> (Bogor), ruled the colony on behalf of the Dutch crown under constitutional regulations such as the Government Regulation of 1854.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest securely dated record naming a king of Sunda is the Kebon Kopi II inscription near <b>Bogor</b>, generally read as dated to 932, and the Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions of 1030 near Sukabumi record a ruler called Sri Jayabupati who declared a stretch of river sacred and forbade…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
